# Record 703: local factors, regulators, and Artin functions.
item(703,'P001355','theorem','The Euler product and completed Riemann zeta function',r'Beilinson lists the Euler product, meromorphic continuation with a single pole at 1, and the symmetric completion \(\pi^{-s/2}\Gamma(s/2)\zeta(s)\).',r'The Euler product converges absolutely for \(\operatorname{Re}s>1\). The zeta function continues meromorphically to \(\mathbb C\), with one simple pole at 1, and \(\Lambda(s)=\pi^{-s/2}\Gamma(s/2)\zeta(s)\) satisfies \(\Lambda(s)=\Lambda(1-s)\). The completed function itself has poles at both 0 and 1; only \(\tfrac12s(s-1)\Lambda(s)\) is entire.',r'These are classical facts reviewed through the local-global integral method in \citet[Sections~2--4]{kudla2003tate}. Tate\textquoteright s thesis circulated in 1950 and was printed in 1967; the source distinguishes those dates.',('kudla2003tate',),correctness='correct')
item(703,'P001355-P001356','construction','Archimedean and p-adic local zeta integrals',r'The Gamma factor is described as a Mellin transform of a Gaussian; the nonarchimedean analogue uses the characteristic function of \(\mathbb Z_p\setminus\{0\}\), called an open subgroup.',r'With additive normalization \(e^{-\pi x^2}\) and multiplicative measure \(dx/|x|\), the real local integral is \(\int_{\mathbb R^\times}e^{-\pi x^2}|x|^s\,dx/|x|=\pi^{-s/2}\Gamma(s/2)\). With \(\operatorname{vol}(\mathbb Z_p^\times)=1\), \(\int_{\mathbb Q_p^\times}1_{\mathbb Z_p}(x)|x|_p^s\,d^\times x=(1-p^{-s})^{-1}\) for \(\operatorname{Re}s>0\). The set \(\mathbb Z_p\setminus\{0\}\) is not a multiplicative subgroup.',r'\citet[local zeta integrals, Sections~2--3]{kudla2003tate} records these factors and the Haar conventions. Kirillov\textquoteright s volume analogy is heuristic: the volume of an \(s\)-ball has a different Gamma expression, even under analytic continuation in dimension.',('kudla2003tate',),correctness='false as written')
item(703,'P001356','theorem','Trivial zeros and Bernoulli special values',r'The functional equation gives simple zeros at negative even integers, and \(\zeta(1-n)=-B_n/n\) is stated at negative arguments.',r'For \(n\ge1\), \(\zeta(-2n)=0\) and the zero is simple. For integers \(m\ge2\), \(\zeta(1-m)=-B_m/m\), using \(B_1=-1/2\); the omitted \(m=1\) case must be handled separately because \(\zeta(0)=-1/2\). The simplicity at negative even integers uses the functional equation together with nonvanishing of the positive real zeta value.',r'The analytic continuation in \citet{kudla2003tate} and the Bernoulli calculation reproduced in \citet[Section~2]{gelbart2012reciprocal} support the corrected conventions. These facts predate the lecture.',('kudla2003tate','gelbart2012reciprocal'))
item(703,'P001356','method','Regularized power sums and the choice of local parameter',r'The notes write \(\zeta(-n)=\sum k^n=(t\,d/dt)^n(1-t)^{-1}|_{t=1}\), clarified as the first regular term.',r'The divergent power sum cannot be evaluated by ordinary substitution. A compatible finite-part prescription uses \(t=e^{-u}\) and the constant term at \(u=0\) of \(\sum_{k\ge1}k^n e^{-ku}\); that constant is \(\zeta(-n)\). Taking instead the constant Laurent coefficient in \(t-1\) is a different operation and generally gives another answer. The regularization parameter must be part of the statement.',r'The Bernoulli generating-function method underlying the special-value formula is explicit in \citet[Section~2]{gelbart2012reciprocal}. The notebook does not specify a parameter; its abbreviated equality is therefore not literally correct as an ordinary series or a coordinate-free finite part.',('gelbart2012reciprocal',),correctness='false as written',match='partial')
item(703,'P001356','theorem','Borel regulators and derivatives at negative even integers',r'The corrected line relates \(\zeta^{\prime}(-2n)\) to the covolume of the image of \(K_{4n+1}(\mathbb Z)\) in \(\mathbb R\).',r'For \(n\ge1\), the torsion-free part of \(K_{4n+1}(\mathbb Z)\) has rank one. Under the Borel regulator with the corresponding Tate normalization, its image is a lattice in \(\mathbb R\), and the nonzero leading zeta-value \(\zeta^{\prime}(-2n)\) agrees with its regulator covolume up to a nonzero rational factor and the declared orientation convention. Torsion is killed by the real regulator.',r'\citet[Section~6, especially~6.2--6.4]{borel1977zeta} proves the commensurability statement; the paper was received in November 1976 and published in 1977. The notebook\textquoteright s replacement of \(\zeta(1-2n)\) by a derivative at \(-2n\) is mathematically meaningful and should be preserved.',('borel1977zeta',),correctness='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(703,'P001356','construction','p-adic interpolation and the missing measure regularization',r'The displayed identity makes \((1-p^{n-1})\zeta(1-n)\) a moment of one \(\mathbb Z_p\)-valued measure on \(\mathbb Z_p^\times\), with no auxiliary factor.',r'The Kubota--Leopoldt construction interpolates Euler-factor-removed zeta or Dirichlet L-values on the appropriate components of p-adic weight space. The zeta branch has a pole and is naturally a pseudomeasure; a bounded measure requires a regularizing factor. For example, in the convention of the cited source and even \(n\ge2\), Mazur\textquoteright s bounded measure satisfies \(\int x^{n-1}d\mu_{1,c}=-(1-c^{-n})(1-p^{n-1})\zeta(1-n)\), where \(c>1\) is prime to \(p\).',r'The original interpolation is \citet{kubotaleopoldt1964}; the explicit bounded-measure formula and its distinction from the unbounded Bernoulli distribution are read in \citet[equations~(2.1), (2.12)--(2.13)]{gelbart2012reciprocal}. The notebook omits both the regularization and the character-component conventions.',('kubotaleopoldt1964','gelbart2012reciprocal'),correctness='false as written')
item(703,'P001356','definition','Components of p-adic weight space',r'The units are split into \((1+p\mathbb Z_p)\) and the \((p-1)\)-st roots of unity, giving \(p-1\) components of the character space.',r'For odd \(p\), \(\mathbb Z_p^\times=\mu_{p-1}\times(1+p\mathbb Z_p)\). Continuous p-adic characters split according to their finite character on \(\mu_{p-1}\); each component is parametrized analytically by the character on the pro-p factor. For \(p=2\), the corresponding decomposition is \(\{\pm1\}\times(1+4\mathbb Z_2)\), so the written odd-prime formula must not be used unchanged.',r'\citet[discussion of weight space and the Iwasawa algebra]{gelbart2012reciprocal} makes the character decomposition explicit in the interpolation construction. The prime restriction is missing from the notes.',('gelbart2012reciprocal',))
item(703,'P001356','conjecture','Riemann hypothesis and prime distribution',r'The final two numbered topics are RH and its connection with the distribution of primes, without an attempted proof.',r'The precise named conjecture is \(\operatorname{Re}\rho=1/2\) for every nontrivial zero \(\rho\) of \(\zeta\). Explicit-formula estimates relate the zero locations to the error in prime-counting functions; the prime number theorem by itself is much weaker than RH.',r'This repeats the conjecture behind record 700. \citet{clay2026riemann} lists RH as unsolved; no resolution is inferred from the adjacent exposition of known analytic continuation.',('clay2026riemann',),cls='C',problem='GP-1986-61',worth=r'Its role is to identify the common unsolved target of the two lectures; an attempted proof is outside a bounded critical annotation.',status=r'Open as of 12 November 2026; the notebook gives a topic heading, not a new equivalent criterion or claimed solution.')
item(703,'P001357','definition','Finite-image Artin representations and ramified Euler factors',r'A continuous complex representation of the absolute Galois group is localized at primes; inertia and Frobenius are introduced, followed by the unramified determinant factor.',r'A continuous finite-dimensional representation \(\rho:G_{\mathbb Q}\to GL(V)\) with the usual complex topology has finite image. It is unramified outside finitely many primes. At every prime, the Artin factor is \(L_p(\rho,s)=\det(1-p^{-s}\rho(\mathrm{Fr}_p)|V^{I_p})^{-1}\), with a consistent arithmetic-Frobenius convention. The choice of decomposition group changes the data by conjugacy. The notebook formula on all of \(V\) applies at unramified primes.',r'\citet[Introduction]{gunhazrasahu2025artin} gives the full inertia-invariant definition and finite-Galois setup. The formulation is classical; the 2025 paper is used to verify the exact general convention and current status, not as the origin of Artin theory.',('gunhazrasahu2025artin',))
item(703,'P001357-P001358','theorem','Dirichlet and Dedekind examples of Artin formalism',r'One-dimensional characters give Dirichlet functions; inducing the trivial character from \(G_K\) gives \(\zeta_K\); direct sums multiply L-functions.',r'For a finite-order one-dimensional character of \(G_{\mathbb Q}\), the Artin L-function is the primitive Dirichlet L-function associated with its conductor. For any number field \(K\), \(L(\operatorname{Ind}_{G_K}^{G_{\mathbb Q}}1,s)=\zeta_K(s)\). Moreover \(L(\rho_1\oplus\rho_2,s)=L(\rho_1,s)L(\rho_2,s)\). Using an imprimitive character modulo a larger integer removes extra Euler factors and must be distinguished from the primitive Artin function.',r'\citet[Section~1]{kudla2003tate} explains primitive and imprimitive characters, while \citet[Section~2]{mantillasoler2019equivalence} gives the induced Galois representation for the Dedekind zeta function.',('kudla2003tate','mantillasoler2019equivalence'))
item(703,'P001358','theorem','An Artin L-function determines the representation but not the field',r'The notes explicitly distinguish recovering \(\rho\) from \(L(\rho,s)\) and the failure to recover \(K\) from \(\zeta_K(s)\).',r'Finite-image complex representations of \(G_{\mathbb Q}\) with identical Artin L-functions are isomorphic: equality of Dirichlet coefficients determines unramified Frobenius traces, Chebotarev determines the finite-group character, and complex finite-group representations are semisimple. However nonconjugate subgroups can induce isomorphic permutation representations, yielding nonisomorphic arithmetically equivalent number fields.',r'\citet[Theorem~2.5, Corollary~2.8 and Section~3 examples]{mantillasoler2019equivalence} gives this argument and explicit field phenomena, tracing the arithmetic-equivalence criterion to Perlis (1977) and Gassmann. The later paper confirms rather than originates the notebook\textquoteright s distinction.',('mantillasoler2019equivalence',),correctness='correct')
item(703,'P001358','conjecture','Artin holomorphy',r'All complex Artin functions are said to be meromorphic; for nontrivial irreducible \(\rho\), holomorphy everywhere is conjectured.',r'Brauer induction gives meromorphic continuation to \(\mathbb C\) of every finite-image complex Artin L-function. Artin\textquoteright s conjecture asserts that \(L(\rho,s)\) is entire when \(\rho\) is nontrivial and irreducible. Trivial summands must be excluded because they contribute a Dedekind-zeta pole. Monomial representations and many further classes are known, but the general conjecture remains open.',r'\citet[Introduction and Theorems in Section~1]{gunhazrasahu2025artin}, published 17 February 2025, explicitly distinguishes the classical meromorphic-continuation theorem from the still-open holomorphy assertion and gives new partial criteria.',('gunhazrasahu2025artin',),cls='C',problem='GP-1986-64',worth=r'The exact finite-image formulation and the boundary between Brauer meromorphy and entire continuation deserve preservation; no general proof is attempted here.',correctness='correct',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, meromorphic continuation is established for all complex Artin representations, while the general nontrivial irreducible holomorphy conjecture is unresolved.')
item(703,'P001358','remark','The scope of the l-adic continuation question',r'For l-adic representations, the notes say that even meromorphic continuation is unknown.',r'This sentence cannot apply uniformly to arbitrary continuous \(\ell\)-adic representations without additional conditions. To formulate the intended arithmetic problem one needs, at a minimum, a representation unramified outside finitely many places, suitable algebraic Frobenius eigenvalues and a complex realization; the geometric or motivic setting supplies the usual conjectural analytic continuation problem. These hypotheses are not stated in the notebook.',r'The adjoining Artin discussion, verified in \citet{gunhazrasahu2025artin}, concerns finite-image complex representations and cannot settle this broader question. No specific motivic representation or exact conjecture is identified here.',('gunhazrasahu2025artin',),cls='F',correctness='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',development='none',confidence='medium',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, the unqualified sentence is not a well-defined universal theorem or conjecture. Important geometric cases are known, but none is specified sufficiently here for a precise fate assignment.')
item(703,'P001358','theorem','The completed Artin functional equation',r'The last formula relates \(L^*(\rho,s)\) to \(L^*(\rho^*,1-s)\), with an epsilon factor containing Gauss sums.',r'For a finite-image complex Artin representation over \(\mathbb Q\), let \(a_\pm\) be the dimensions of the \(\pm1\)-eigenspaces of complex conjugation and \(A(\rho)\) its Artin conductor. With \(\Gamma_{\mathbb R}(s)=\pi^{-s/2}\Gamma(s/2)\), set \(\Lambda(\rho,s)=A(\rho)^{s/2}\Gamma_{\mathbb R}(s)^{a_+}\Gamma_{\mathbb R}(s+1)^{a_-}L(\rho,s)\). Then \(\Lambda(\rho,s)=W(\rho)\Lambda(\rho^*,1-s)\) meromorphically, where \(|W(\rho)|=1\). If the conductor is omitted from the completion, the epsilon factor depends on \(s\).',r'The Artin analytic framework in \citet{gunhazrasahu2025artin} distinguishes known functional equations from conjectural holomorphy; the local-global normalization is illustrated in \citet[Sections~3--4]{kudla2003tate}. The notebook has not fixed which conductor factor is absorbed in \(L^*\).',('gunhazrasahu2025artin','kudla2003tate'),match='partial')
problem('GP-1986-64','Artin holomorphy for nontrivial irreducible complex representations',r'For every nontrivial irreducible continuous finite-dimensional complex representation \(\rho\) of the absolute Galois group of a number field, is its Artin L-function entire?',['1986-703.12'],r'Open in general as of 12 November 2026; Brauer induction proves meromorphic continuation, and abelian, monomial and numerous other cases are known. Recent partial holomorphy criteria do not establish the universal conjecture.',['gunhazrasahu2025artin'])
next(p for p in problems if p['id']=='GP-1986-61')['occurrences'].append('1986-703.8')
correction('P001355-P001358','records 703 and 704','catalogue spans overlap; record 703 stops at P001356','Attribute the explicit Beilinson heading and continuation through P001358 to 703, and the preceding Gelfand proposals to 704.','Original scan P001355 clearly separates the two named speakers. Catalogue metadata are retained in talks.json.','both','provenance')
correction('P001356','p-adic Gaussian analogue','Z_p minus zero called an open subgroup','It is an open multiplicative submonoid of Q_p^*; the open compact subgroup is Z_p^*. The zeta test function is 1_(Z_p).','The manuscript writes Z_p without a unit star; negative valuations show failure of closure under inversion.')
correction('P001356','regularized power sum','evaluate the first regular term at t=1','Specify the parameter t=e^(-u) and take the constant term in u, or declare another regularization.','A Laurent finite part changes under nonlinear change of parameter; ordinary substitution diverges.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P001356','p-adic zeta measure','one Z_p-valued measure without regularizing factor','Use a pseudomeasure for the zeta branch or a bounded Mazur measure with its auxiliary factor and character conventions.','Gelbart et al. equations (2.1), (2.12), (2.13) explicitly distinguish bounded measures from Bernoulli distributions.')
correction('P001356','unit decomposition','Z_p^*=(1+pZ_p) times roots of unity of order p-1','State p odd; at p=2 use {+1,-1} times (1+4Z_2).','The two-adic unit group has a separate order-two factor.')
