# Record 704: proposals, not automatic new open problems.
item(704,'P001355','remark','Fractional-dimensional measures and representation parameters',r'Gelfand mentions a work on integral fractional-linear transformations of the upper half-plane and sets carrying measures of intermediate Hausdorff dimension, in connection with a series of representations.',r'The notes identify neither the work nor the acting subgroup, invariant or quasi-invariant measure, and representation functor. A relation between dimension and a principal or complementary-series parameter is a plausible theme, but there is no unique theorem or conjecture to reconstruct from the sentence.',r'Searches for the 1986--1987 combinations of Gelfand, Hausdorff measures, fractional-linear actions and representation series did not recover an exact source. The nearby multifractal literature is only thematic and is deliberately not substituted for the missing reference.',(),cls='F',correctness='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',development='none',confidence='low',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, the exact referent remains unidentified. No claim of mathematical nondevelopment follows from this negative identification result.')
item(704,'P001355','conjecture','Factors arising from representations of infinite-dimensional groups',r'The word ``hypothesis'' introduces the claim that factors arise from representations of infinite-dimensional groups.',r'To turn this into a mathematical conjecture one must specify the class of groups, representations, generated von Neumann algebras, factor types and any universality assertion. Without those restrictions the statement is either a broad observation or a tautological realization using unitary groups; the notebook gives no discriminating condition.',r'No precise publication match is asserted. The statement is preserved as a research-program remark adjacent to factor classification, not converted into an invented universal classification problem.',(),cls='F',correctness='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',development='none',confidence='low',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, this formulation is insufficient to identify a specific open question or a theorem with a determinate fate.')
item(704,'P001355','theorem','Uniqueness of the hyperfinite type III1 factor',r'The notes say physics produces factors of type \(III_\infty\), of which exactly one is hyperfinite.',r'The recognizable theorem is uniqueness, up to isomorphism, of the injective (equivalently, in the separable setting, hyperfinite) type \(III_1\) factor with separable predual. There is no standard Connes type \(III_\infty\); the subscript \(\infty\) also occurs in the conventional name \(R_\infty\) for the unique factor, which may explain the notation.',r'\citet[Section~2]{haagerup1987bicentralizer} completes the uniqueness proof; the paper was received on 21 January 1985 and printed in April 1987. \citet[publisher description of the 1985--1986 lectures]{wright1989uniqueness} confirms circulation before the March 1987 meeting. This is therefore E, not a new conjecture by Gelfand.',('haagerup1987bicentralizer','wright1989uniqueness'),correctness='false as written',reconstruction=r'Type \(III_1\), separability and injectivity are the editorial reconstruction of the recognizable uniqueness statement. The manuscript\textquoteright s type label is retained in notes rather than silently corrected.',confidence='high')
item(704,'P001355','remark','Factors, attractors, chaos, and characteristic functions',r'A list juxtaposes factors, attractors, chaos, Hausdorff measures and characteristic functions.',r'The list records proposed connections among operator algebras, dynamics and fractal measures. It specifies no map, equality, invariant or question; each named theory has many inequivalent ways of interacting with the others.',r'The local context supports a thematic reading only. Neither the type-\(III_1\) theorem nor a later general theory of multifractals can serve as an exact solution of an unspecified list.',(),cls='F',correctness='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',development='none',confidence='low',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, no definite mathematical assertion can be extracted from the list without inventing substantial additional content.')
correction('P001355','factor type','III_infinity','The recognizable uniqueness result concerns type III_1 with separable predual; retain III_infinity as the recorded label.','Original scan confirms infinity. Haagerup 1987 Section 2 proves uniqueness for III_1, conventionally denoted R_infinity.','both','ambiguity')
save()

# Record 705: keep convention corrections separate from new spectral proposals.
item(705,'P001359','method','KdV as a Lax compatibility condition',r'The displayed equation is \(u_t+6uu_x+u_{xxx}=0\), while the spectral equations use \(L=\partial_x^2+u\) and \(A=4\partial_x^3+6u\partial_x+3u_x\). The free wave is \(e^{ikx+4ik^3t}\).',r'For this pair, compatibility \(L_t=[A,L]\) gives \(u_t=6uu_x+u_{xxx}\). A free solution with wave number \(k\) has spectral eigenvalue \(-k^2\) and time factor \(e^{-4ik^3t}\). The standard KdV convention in the first line is recovered by reversing time and making the same change throughout the pair. The potential and eigenfunction must be smooth enough for the differentiations.',r'The inverse-scattering and dressing framework is classical and is reviewed in \citet{zakharovshabat1974scheme}. The notebook combines two opposite time conventions. This is a correction of a faithfully recorded formula, not a new integrability claim.',('zakharovshabat1974scheme',),correctness='false as written',match='partial')
item(705,'P001359-P001360','construction','Marchenko dressing and its solvability conditions',r'The integral equation is \(K(x,z)+F(x+z)+\int K(x,s)F(s+z)\,ds=0\), with three proposed integration conventions, \(F_t=8F_{\xi\xi\xi}\), and \(u=2(d/dx)K(x,x)\).',r'In the right Marchenko convention the integral is over \([x,\infty)\). Assume sufficient smoothness and decay to differentiate and integrate by parts, and invertibility of the corresponding integral operator so that the normalized equation has a unique solution. The diagonal formula then produces the potential for the consistently signed Lax system. The left and half-difference prescriptions require their own boundary or formal-integral conventions; they are not unconditional solutions for every function \(F\).',r'\citet{zakharovshabat1974scheme} develops the general dressing scheme cited explicitly in the lecture. The notebook omits the unique-solvability and boundary assumptions and carries over the KdV time-sign inconsistency. Its extension to growing exponentials is a formal prescription, not a convergent integral on an infinite interval.',('zakharovshabat1974scheme',),correctness='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial')
item(705,'P001360','construction','The KP extension needs a nonlocal coefficient in its Lax pair',r'The kernel condition becomes \(F_{xx}-F_{zz}=\alpha F_y\), but the displayed wave equation has coefficient 2 and retains the one-dimensional time operator unchanged.',r'A consistent KP pair is \((\alpha\partial_y-\partial_x^2-u)\psi=0\) and \((\partial_t-4\partial_x^3-6u\partial_x-3u_x-3\alpha w)\psi=0\), with \(w_x=u_y\). Compatibility yields \((u_t-6uu_x-u_{xxx})_x=3\alpha^2u_{yy}\). Boundary data fix the integration constant in \(w\). Omitting \(w\) imposes extra restrictions and does not give generic KP solutions.',r'The multidimensional dressing construction in \citet{zakharovshabat1974scheme} is the precise antecedent acknowledged by the notes. The reconstructed pair fixes a declared convention; it is not a verbatim restoration of an absent notebook term.',('zakharovshabat1974scheme',),reconstruction=r'The auxiliary potential \(w\), its boundary convention and a consistent \(\alpha\)-normalization are supplied editorially.',correctness='false as written',match='partial')
item(705,'P001360-P001361','method','The normalized nonlocal d-bar problem',r'The Fourier-transformed kernel leads to \(\bar\partial_\lambda\chi=\int\chi(\eta)T(\eta,\lambda)\,d\eta\,d\bar\eta\), with \(\chi(\infty)=1\); the coefficient of \(\lambda^{-1}\) determines \(u\).',r'Choose an area-form convention and a kernel for which the normalized nonlocal \(\bar\partial\)-problem is uniquely solvable, for example in a Fredholm setting with no normalized homogeneous solution. Its Cauchy transform must use the matching distributional normalization \(\bar\partial(1/z)=\pi\delta_0\). Differentiating a uniquely solvable problem with compatible parameter dependence supplies the auxiliary linear equations. The potential is extracted from the first coefficient at infinity after fixing the oscillatory convention.',r'\citet[Section~1]{zakharovmanakov1984multidimensional} explicitly makes uniqueness the central dressing hypothesis and announces the nonlocal d-bar extension. \citet{fokaszakharov1992dressing} later treats this method directly. The original lecture is identifiable as this construction; an arbitrary kernel does not automatically yield a nonsingular solution.',('zakharovmanakov1984multidimensional','fokaszakharov1992dressing'),match='partial',verification='read the result')
item(705,'P001361-P001362','construction','Commuting rational flows and Riemann-surface dressing data',r'The kernel evolves by \(T_{x_i}=I_i(\eta)T-TI_i(\lambda)\), with mutually commuting rational matrix functions; the notes also allow functions on a Riemann surface.',r'For compatible commuting multipliers, the kernel flows commute. A normalized uniquely solvable dressing problem then generates compatible dressed operators. On a compact Riemann surface the meromorphic functions, allowed poles, normalization divisor and any vector bundle must be specified. The polynomial choices for KP must agree with the same spectral and time conventions used to define the Fourier transform.',r'\citet{zakharovmanakov1984multidimensional} supplies the established multidimensional dressing framework. The recorded cubic flow \(4\lambda^3\), without the imaginary factor dictated by the earlier oscillatory wave, cannot be matched to that earlier convention without a change of time variable. The unrestricted Riemann-surface sentence is a programmatic extension rather than a complete existence theorem.',('zakharovmanakov1984multidimensional',),match='partial')
item(705,'P001362','method','First-order differentiation in a variable spectral parameter',r'The new proposal is \(L_i=\partial_{x_i}+K_i(\lambda)+f_i(\lambda)\partial_\lambda\), with rational coefficients and moving poles; the gravitational example is attributed to Belinsky and Zakharov.',r'The compatibility equation for such operators contains both matrix curvature and the commutator of the scalar spectral vector fields. Once an ansatz for poles and residues is fixed, its coefficients give nonlinear equations in the deformation variables. The count of equations and unknowns and solvability depend on that ansatz, so they are not universal consequences of writing \([L_i,L_j]=0\).',r'\citet[Introduction, equations~(1.9)--(1.10), and subsequent constructions]{burtsevzakharovmikhailov1987variable} treats inverse scattering with a variable spectral parameter and includes the gravitational motivation. The paper was received on 6 February 1986 and published in March 1987, so this first-order part was circulating before the lecture, even though the notebook labels the wider proposal new.',('burtsevzakharovmikhailov1987variable',),match='partial')
item(705,'P001362','claim','The zero-curvature condition for spectral differential operators',r'For \(L_i=\partial_{x_i}+M_i(\lambda,\partial_\lambda)\), the notes write \([L_i,L_j]=0\) if and only if \([M_i,M_j]=0\).',r'The full condition is \(\partial_{x_i}M_j-\partial_{x_j}M_i+[M_i,M_j]=0\). It reduces to the written commutativity condition only when the \(M_i\) are independent of all deformation variables, or when their cross derivatives cancel for a specified reason. Rationality in \(\lambda\) alone does not remove these terms.',r'This is the elementary compatibility identity used in the variable-spectral-parameter framework of \citet{burtsevzakharovmikhailov1987variable}. The sentence becomes correct for the constant-coefficient-in-\(x\) example that follows, but not for the preceding claim about freely moving poles.',('burtsevzakharovmikhailov1987variable',),correctness='false as written',match='partial')
item(705,'P001362','construction','A corrected commuting pair of matrix differential operators',r'The notes require \(I^2=1\), \(A^2=-1\), \(IA+AI=0\), set \(C=-(I+A)/2\), \(M_1=I\partial_\lambda+A/\lambda\), \(M_2=M_1\partial_\lambda+C/\lambda^2\), but display \(A=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)\).',r'With \(I=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\) and \(A=\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)\), the required matrix relations hold and \([M_1,M_2]=0\) on vector-valued meromorphic functions away from \(\lambda=0\). With the notebook\textquoteright s plus/plus matrix the relations fail and \([M_1,M_2]=2\operatorname{Id}/\lambda^3\).',r'\oursComputed{} The exact operator composition and a second calculation by direct action on polynomial vectors independently verify both conclusions. The second check uses two basis vectors, seven degrees and four nonzero rational spectral values for both matrices, giving 112 exact comparisons. The supplied code and output record these checks. The scan confirms that both displayed off-diagonal signs are positive; this is a faithful mathematical error. The general compatibility method predates the talk, but no exact published priority claim is made for this particular illustrative pair.',('burtsevzakharovmikhailov1987variable',),correctness='false as written',match='partial',development='annotated',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, the bounded algebraic issue is completely checked in this companion. This verification repairs an example; it does not establish the lecture\textquoteright s proposed general classification of nonlinear systems.')
item(705,'P001362','question','Solving the common system for higher spectral differential operators',r'After the commuting example the notes ask to find solutions of \(L_i y=0\), and say that a great deal of computation is required.',r'For the proposed operators containing higher derivatives in the spectral variable, a common-solution problem needs specified coefficient functions, domains, singularities, initial or asymptotic data, and a function class for \(y\). The notebook supplies an algebraic example but no such analytic problem. Formal compatibility alone does not specify a unique solution or an inverse-scattering theorem.',r'\citet{burtsevzakharovmikhailov1987variable} identifies the first-order spectral-derivative background. A bibliographic lead to Zakharov\textquoteright s 1988 conference contribution on commuting operators and a nonlocal d-bar problem was located, but its original text was not recovered; it is not used to certify the unrestricted higher-order proposal.',('burtsevzakharovmikhailov1987variable',),cls='F',correctness='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',development='none',confidence='medium',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, the analytic problem intended here is underdetermined. The verified algebraic example is separated in item 1986-705.8; no general open problem is inferred from the missing formulation.')
item(705,'P001363','method','Degenerating soliton kernels to rational solutions',r'The prescription begins with Gelfand--Levitan--Marchenko equations in the spectral variable, takes \(n\)-soliton solutions, and degenerates them to rational solutions; the next step is to construct the kernels \(K(\lambda,\lambda^{\prime},x_i)\) and \(F(\lambda,\lambda^{\prime},x_i)\).',r'A definite degeneration theorem must specify the nonlinear system, a family of scattering or kernel data, the rescaling and coalescence parameters, and the topology in which the potentials or wave functions converge. None of these limiting data is fixed in this passage. The assertion is therefore a proposed method, rather than a stated theorem for all rational solutions.',r'\citet[Section~3]{zakharovshabat1974scheme} gives explicit finite-rank soliton kernels in the established dressing method. That construction is an antecedent, not an exact publication match for the unspecified spectral-variable rational limit suggested here.',('zakharovshabat1974scheme',),cls='F',correctness='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='read the result',development='none',confidence='medium',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, rational limits of soliton constructions are a recognizable topic, but this notebook passage does not select one equation or degeneration theorem whose precise fate can be assigned.')
item(705,'P001363','claim','The proposed scheme is said to include dissipative systems',r'The single sentence says that dissipative systems are included too.',r'To test this assertion one must define the evolution, the real form, the phase space and a dissipated functional or contraction property. A non-self-adjoint spectral operator by itself is not a definition of dissipative dynamics. The passage gives none of these data.',r'The spectral-parameter extension of \citet{burtsevzakharovmikhailov1987variable} provides related operator machinery, but it does not identify which dissipative system this sentence intends. No exact theorem is assigned on the strength of the shared vocabulary.',('burtsevzakharovmikhailov1987variable',),cls='F',correctness='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',development='none',confidence='low',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, the specific claim remains unidentifiable from the notes. This is a limitation of the recorded statement, not evidence that dissipative integrable models have not been developed.')
item(705,'P001363','construction','Spectral differential operators multiplying the evolution derivatives',r'The next generalization is \(L_i=R_i(\lambda,\partial_\lambda)\partial_{x_i}-M_i(\lambda,\partial_\lambda)\).',r'The displayed operator pencil is well-defined formally once coefficient regularity and an operator ordering are chosen. A compatibility or dressing assertion additionally requires the orders, dependence on the evolution variables, domains, and any invertibility assumptions on \(R_i\). The notebook states the ansatz but supplies no resulting equation or theorem.',r'The inspected first-order predecessor \citet{burtsevzakharovmikhailov1987variable} is a thematic antecedent. The missing conference text cannot be used to claim an exact later treatment of arbitrary \(R_i\) and \(M_i\).',('burtsevzakharovmikhailov1987variable',),cls='F',correctness='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',development='none',confidence='medium',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, the ansatz is clear but its intended mathematical assertion is not. No universal solvability theorem or undeveloped open problem is asserted.')
