# Record 706. Keep the cube at occurrence .3 for the integrated development.
item(706,'P001363','definition','Matrix expectation and the integration contour',r'The matrix integral has weight \(\exp[-(N/\lambda)\operatorname{Tr}V(A)]\), while \(A^*=-A\); the loop observable is \(\psi(t)=N^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}e^{tA}\).',r'A rigorous one-matrix model can be defined on Hermitian matrices with real confining polynomial \(V\) and \(\lambda>0\), normalized by its finite partition function. A skew-Hermitian convention instead needs a compatible contour and potential obtained by \(A=iH\); it is not the same real-eigenvalue integral without changing these data. Formal power-series expectations provide another explicitly distinct interpretation.',r'\citet[Sections~2--3]{brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar} defines the Hermitian planar model and its eigenvalue reduction. The notebook switches between anti-Hermitian gauge fields and real-eigenvalue matrix formulas without declaring the contour change.',('brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar',),reconstruction=r'The confining Hermitian model is the precise setting used for the analytical statements below; it is an editorial choice, not a silent change to the recorded anti-Hermitian line.',correctness='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(706,'P001363-P001364','theorem','The finite-matrix Schwinger--Dyson identity',r'An integral of a total matrix derivative yields a convolution equation. The first formula omits the expectation signs, which the notebook itself restores on the next page.',r'Under the confining assumptions of the preceding item, integration by parts and the Duhamel identity \(D(e^{tA})[H]=\int_0^t e^{sA}He^{(t-s)A}\,ds\) give \[0=\int_0^t\mathbb E[\psi(s)\psi(t-s)]\,ds-\lambda^{-1}V^{\prime}(\partial_t)\mathbb E\psi(t).\] Boundary terms must vanish and the derivative convention must be the Hermitian one. This is an identity of expectations, not an identity for each matrix.',r'The loop-equation method is already present in \citet{brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar}. Its exact finite-size identity precedes any use of large-N factorization. The notebook\textquoteright s later correction is preserved rather than reported as a new editorial discovery.',('brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar',),correctness='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(706,'P001364','problem','Planes through triples of cube vertices',r'Gelfand attributes an elegant problem to T. Alekseevskaya: draw a plane through every three vertices of a three-dimensional cube and count the pieces. The notebook already gives the answer 96.',r'Take the eight vertices of a nondegenerate cube in \(\mathbb R^3\). Form all distinct affine planes through triples of distinct vertices, and count the connected components of the open cube after deleting these planes. There are 20 distinct planes; six are supporting face planes and fourteen cut the interior. The number of components is 96.',r'\oursComputed{} The chapter independently verifies the already recorded answer by exact rational cell enumeration and by the intersection-poset count \(1+14+47+34=96\). It supplies a rational witness for each region. This is a reproducible annotation of an answered notebook problem; no claim of a new open problem or new numerical discovery is made.',(),correctness='correct',match='none found',verification='read the result',speaker='I. M. Gelfand, attributed to T. Alekseevskaya',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, the notebook answer is confirmed by two independent exact methods in this companion. Historical publication priority for this elementary puzzle has not been determined.')
item(706,'P001364','claim','The planar sector for classical matrix symmetry classes',r'The notes write \(O(\infty)=SU(\infty)=Sp(\infty)\) and say the large-size limit is the same for all classical groups.',r'With matching matrix and coupling normalizations, the leading oriented planar diagrams of real, complex and quaternionic invariant matrix models are related. The full theories differ in symmetry factors and nonorientable or subleading terms. There is no equality or isomorphism of the displayed infinite classical groups, and equality of arbitrary matrix-model observables is not implied.',r'The graphical expansion in \citet{bessisitzyksonzuber1980graphical} explains the intended leading-diagram comparison. The literal group equality is shorthand that becomes false if read as a group-theoretic theorem.',('bessisitzyksonzuber1980graphical',),correctness='false as written',match='partial')
item(706,'P001365','theorem','Large-N factorization and closure of the planar equation',r'Normalized trace correlations are replaced by the product of their expectations up to \(O(N^{-2})\).',r'In the unitary invariant formal topological expansion, connected correlators of two normalized traces begin at order \(N^{-2}\). In convergent analytic models this requires hypotheses that ensure the relevant planar limit and fluctuations; it is not a statement about arbitrary random matrices. Under those assumptions the finite Schwinger--Dyson equation closes for the limiting one-trace function.',r'\citet{brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar} develops the planar limit; \citet{bessisitzyksonzuber1980graphical} gives the Euler-characteristic bookkeeping. The same factorization principle appears in earlier occurrence 1982-565.3 and in the reduction discussion of record 698.',('brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar','bessisitzyksonzuber1980graphical'),match='partial')
item(706,'P001365-P001366','construction','The planar resolvent and its quadratic equation',r'The Laplace transform of the trace exponential becomes \(f(z)=N^{-1}\mathbb E\operatorname{Tr}(z-A)^{-1}\), with \(R=f^2-\lambda^{-1}V^{\prime}f\); the next page solves the quadratic using \(-R\) under the root.',r'For a limiting compactly supported eigenvalue measure \(\rho\), \(W(z)=\int(z-x)^{-1}d\rho(x)\) is analytic off its support and satisfies \(W(z)\sim z^{-1}\). The planar equation gives \(R(z)=W(z)^2-\lambda^{-1}V^{\prime}(z)W(z)\), a polynomial fixed by moments and support conditions. Therefore \[W(z)=\frac{V^{\prime}(z)}{2\lambda}-\sqrt{\left(\frac{V^{\prime}(z)}{2\lambda}\right)^2+R(z)},\] with the branch fixed at infinity. A Laplace representation requires its own convergence half-plane.',r'\citet[planar eigenvalue solution]{brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar} supplies the established algebraic-resolvent method. Relative to the notebook\textquoteright s own definition of \(R\), its radicand sign is incorrect.',('brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar',),correctness='false as written')
item(706,'P001365-P001366','method','Boundary values, density, and the one-cut ansatz',r'The discontinuity of \(f\) is called its imaginary part without a factor \(i\); a single square root \(\sqrt{(z-a)(z-b)}\) is then used.',r'For a real density on its support, \(W_+-W_-=-2\pi i\rho\), \(\operatorname{Im}W_+=(W_+-W_-)/(2i)\), and \(W_+^2-W_-^2=4i\operatorname{Re}W_+\operatorname{Im}W_+\). The form \(W=V^{\prime}/(2\lambda)+M(z)\sqrt{(z-a)(z-b)}\) assumes a one-cut solution. Polynomial \(M\), endpoints, normalization and positivity are constrained by the potential; multi-cut phases need more branch points.',r'\citet[Sections~2--3]{brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar} develops the one-cut solution and its selection conditions. Zakharevich\textquoteright s boundary-value intervention on P001366 fits this scalar Riemann--Hilbert reformulation; it does not remove the support assumptions.',('brezinitzyksonparisizuber1978planar',),correctness='false as written')
item(706,'P001366','definition','Wilson loops as gauge-invariant parallel transport traces',r'The matrix exponential is replaced by \(W(c)=N^{-1}\operatorname{Tr}P\exp\oint_c A_\mu dx_\mu\).',r'For a smooth connection in a unitary representation and a piecewise smooth closed path, path ordering defines its parallel transport. Its normalized trace is invariant under gauge transformations because the holonomy transforms by conjugation at the basepoint. For quantum fields this formal observable requires a regulator and, in continuum limits, renormalization depending on path singularities.',r'\citet[Sections~1 and~3]{makeenko2008cusped} reviews this construction and the additional ultraviolet issues at cusps and intersections. It is established gauge-theory notation, not a claim that an unregularized four-dimensional path integral has been constructed.',('makeenko2008cusped',))
item(706,'P001366-P001367','method','The Makeenko--Migdal loop equation and its scope',r'The lecture proposes repeating the matrix integration-by-parts argument for the gauge-field functional integral and ends with an abbreviated functional derivative.',r'The formal gauge-field Schwinger--Dyson identity relates an area derivative of a Wilson loop to a contact term splitting the contour at coincident points. In the planar limit, factorization replaces the two-loop expectation by a product. A precise continuum equation must specify regularization, renormalization and treatment of cusps and intersections; deriving this identity is not a solution of four-dimensional Yang--Mills theory.',r'The original equation was published in \citet{makeenkomigdal1979exact}; \citet[Section~3.1, equation~(3.2), and Section~3.3]{makeenko2008cusped} gives the regularized interpretation and its limitations. The lecture is an exposition of the established loop-equation method.',('makeenkomigdal1979exact','makeenko2008cusped'),match='partial')
item(706,'P001363; P001366','remark','The proposed noncommutative analogue of Karasev symbols',r'The talk is introduced as a noncommutative analogue of Karasev\textquoteright s symbol. Later it says that the components of a gauge field cannot be diagonalized simultaneously.',r'The obstruction to simultaneous diagonalization of noncommuting matrices is definite. The intended analogue of a Karasev symbol, however, has no stated domain, product, map or asymptotic parameter beyond large matrix size. It cannot be uniquely identified with a particular deformation-quantization construction from this phrase.',r'The matrix and loop methods are identified in the preceding items. They supply thematic context, but no exact symbol calculus is reconstructed or credited on the strength of this introductory phrase.',(),cls='F',correctness='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',development='none',confidence='low',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, the particular proposed symbol calculus is not identifiable from the record; the matrix loop equations themselves are established.')
correction('P001363','matrix reality convention','A^*=-A followed by real-eigenvalue resolvent formulas','Declare an anti-Hermitian contour and transformed potential, or explicitly reconstruct the Hermitian model.','The manuscript anti-Hermitian sign is faithful; later real-cut formulas require a convention change.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P001364','cube-problem parenthesis','vypisal?','The scan reads iziaschnaia, meaning elegant, in the parenthesis after the attribution.','Direct inspection of P001364; the word is a description of the problem, not a copying instruction.','both','transcription')
correction('P001365','imaginary part and jump','Im f=(f_+-f_-)/2; f_+^2-f_-^2=2 Im f Re f','Use Im f=(f_+-f_-)/(2i) and f_+^2-f_-^2=4i Im f Re f under usual conventions.','The two notebook equations are mutually inconsistent even if Im is redefined as a half-jump.')
correction('P001366','resolvent square root','sqrt((Vprime/(2lambda))^2-R)','With R=f^2-(Vprime/lambda)f the radicand is (Vprime/(2lambda))^2+R.','The preceding definition fixes the sign algebraically; the original evidence block confirms minus R is in the manuscript.')
save()

# Record 707: local definitions, global theorems and string measures are separate.
item(707,'P001369','definition','Super Riemann surfaces as nonintegrable odd distributions',r'The local odd derivative is \(D=\partial_\theta+\theta\partial_z\); superconformal coordinate changes preserve it up to multiplication by a function.',r'A complex super Riemann surface is a complex supermanifold of dimension \(1|1\) with a rank \(0|1\) subbundle \(\mathcal D\subset T\) such that the bracket induces an isomorphism \(\mathcal D\otimes\mathcal D\to T/\mathcal D\). Locally \(D^2=\partial_z\), and the rescaling factor for a change of generator is invertible and even.',r'This definition was established in the pre-1987 superstring literature. \citet[Section~2.1]{witten2012superriemann} gives the invariant definition and its local normal form; \citet[Section~2]{cranerabin1988uniformization} states the coordinate version used by the lecture.',('witten2012superriemann','cranerabin1988uniformization'),correctness='correct')
item(707,'P001369','construction','The coordinate condition for superconformal maps',r'One even and one odd analytic function parametrize the coordinate changes. The ordinary analogy writes \(\widetilde\partial=F\partial\), \(F=\partial\widetilde z/\partial z\).',r'For a supercoordinate change \((z,\theta)\mapsto(z_1,\theta_1)\), the superconformal condition is \(Dz_1=\theta_1D\theta_1\), with invertible even \(D\theta_1\); then \(D=(D\theta_1)D_1\). In the ordinary analogy \(\partial_{z_1}=(\partial z_1/\partial z)^{-1}\partial_z\). The notebook has inverted the ordinary Jacobian factor.',r'\citet[Section~2, equations~(2.5)--(2.8)]{cranerabin1988uniformization} gives the exact transformation law and the even/odd parametrization. The missing reciprocal in the ordinary comparison is a faithful notebook error.',('cranerabin1988uniformization',),correctness='false as written')
item(707,'P001369','theorem','The local dimension of supermoduli',r'The corrected dimension in the notebook is \((3g-3|2g-2)\).',r'For a smooth unpunctured super Riemann surface of genus \(g\ge2\), the supermoduli stack has local dimension \(3g-3|2g-2\). Its reduced data include a spin structure, and finite automorphisms require a stack or orbifold interpretation. Genus 0 and 1 require automorphism terms; punctures alter the dimension.',r'\citet[Section~2.2, equations~(2.24)--(2.27)]{witten2012superriemann} derives the count from deformation cohomology. \citet[Introduction and Section~3]{cranerabin1988uniformization} distinguishes the already known local index count from its global super-Teichm\"uller construction, published in 1988. The bare dimension assertion is therefore E.',('witten2012superriemann','cranerabin1988uniformization'))
item(707,'P001369','question','Uniformization of super Riemann surfaces',r'Asked for a Riemann mapping analogue, Schwarz answers that a simply connected underlying surface reduces to a standard model.',r'For the DeWitt-type super Riemann surfaces of the cited theorem, with trivial topology in the nilpotent directions, uniformization uses the supersphere, superplane or upper half-superplane, together with superconformal covering transformations. The topology assumption and supermanifold category matter: simple connectedness of the body alone is not a complete theorem for every broader Rogers supermanifold.',r'\citet[Sections~2--5]{cranerabin1988uniformization} proves uniformization and the associated Teichm\"uller description in the stated category, publishing in 1988; its Section~6 explicitly separates a more general Rogers-topology conjecture. This precisely qualifies the short answer rather than asserting a universal category-independent result.',('cranerabin1988uniformization',),cls='B',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The DeWitt topology and the distinction between individual surfaces and families are supplied from the theorem; the notebook does not specify them.')
item(707,'P001369-P001370','theorem','Riemann--Roch and nonfree cohomology modules',r'Gelfand asks for the function ring on a compact supercurve. Schwarz warns that kernels of equations over a Grassmann algebra need not be free, and only then discusses Riemann--Roch dimensions.',r'For a family over an odd base, cohomology is a module over the base algebra and need not be free. Ranks can be used only on loci where the relevant direct images are locally free and commute with base change. The correct general invariant is the cohomological index or determinant/Berezinian of cohomology, with parity retained; a numerical dimension formula does not automatically describe the full function algebra.',r'The warning in the notebook is substantive and should be preserved. The general supercurve theory is identified by \citet{roslyschwarzvoronov1988geometry}, published in March 1988; its original full text was not recovered, so an exact theorem-number match is not claimed. \citet[Section~3.4]{witten2012superriemann} verifies the cohomological formulation and its qualifications.',('roslyschwarzvoronov1988geometry','witten2012superriemann'),cls='A',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium')
item(707,'P001370','construction','Super Abelian differentials, primitives, and periods',r'Sections transforming like \(D\Phi\) are called Abelian differentials. Primitives on the universal cover give periods, followed by Jacobian and Picard constructions.',r'The superconformal distribution determines the appropriate line bundle of differentials and its Berezinian interpretation. Periods are defined from compatible closed differential representatives and cycles of the reduced surface. A normalized super period matrix has additional spin and nonsingularity conditions and can develop poles when the relevant Dirac operator acquires zero modes; it is not an everywhere regular coordinate on all supermoduli.',r'\citet{roslyschwarzvoronov1988geometry} is the near-contemporary publication match for this theory. \citet[Section~8, especially the end of~8.3]{witten2012superriemann} supplies a readable exact account of periods and the pole qualification. The 1988 paper is verified at metadata/abstract level; the later technical account was opened and read.',('roslyschwarzvoronov1988geometry','witten2012superriemann'),cls='A',match='partial',verification='read the result')
item(707,'P001370','theorem','The super Selberg trace formula',r'The string partition function is related to a superanalogue of Selberg zeta, described as joint work with Manin.',r'The identifiable result is a Selberg-type trace formula for discrete groups acting on the complex upper half-superplane, relating spectral data of super differential operators to geometric conjugacy-class data. Applications concern determinant factors in fermionic-string measures. The notebook specifies neither the test-function class nor the precise super zeta product, so those formulas are not reconstructed from this sentence.',r'\citet{baranovmaninfrolovschwarz1987selberg}, published in September 1987, is an exact subject and collaboration match. The publisher abstract explicitly states the trace formula and its measure applications. Verification here is bibliographic and abstract-level; no uninspected detailed trace identity is asserted.',('baranovmaninfrolovschwarz1987selberg',),cls='A',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='high',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, the announced trace-formula construction is published. The original full proof was not inspected in this run, and the notebook contains too little to compare normalizations.')
item(707,'P001370','construction','Odd frames and the superconformal metric gauge',r'An odd field \(\widehat e\) satisfying \(\{\widehat e,\overline{\widehat e}\}=\tau\widehat e+\overline\tau\overline{\widehat e}\) is said to admit local coordinates with \(\widehat e=\lambda D\).',r'The bracket condition is a local integrability condition for the appropriate complex superconformal distribution. A nondegeneracy condition on its self-bracket and an invertible scale \(\lambda\) are also needed. The resulting frame description is local and carries gauge freedom; a single arbitrary odd vector field on a \(2|2\) supermanifold is not automatically a supermetric.',r'\citet[Sections~3.5--3.6]{witten2012superriemann} explains the relation between superconformal geometry, supergravity and frame gauges. The local formulation is part of the pre-existing supergravity framework; the notebook suppresses the nondegeneracy and reality conventions.',('witten2012superriemann',),match='partial')
item(707,'P001370-P001371','construction','Determinant expressions for bosonic and fermionic string measures',r'The notes contrast a scalar determinant power \(-13\) for the bosonic string with \(-5\) for the fermionic string, followed by ghost determinants and a reference to Baranov, Schwarz and Voronov.',r'The exponents reflect Gaussian matter-field counts in the respective critical dimensions, but a genuine string measure also specifies ghost complexes, fermionic Pfaffians or superdeterminants, zero-mode removal, spin structures, determinant lines and integration over the appropriate moduli. A bare product of unprimed scalar determinants is only schematic. The local quadratic action also needs its pairing and conjugation convention.',r'\citet{roslyschwarzvoronov1988geometry} is a direct near-contemporary match for the super-Laplacian and measure program. The established bosonic determinant-line background includes \citet{beilinsonmanin1986mumford}. The notebook does not state enough to identify every determinant factor or to infer convergence of the integrated string amplitude.',('roslyschwarzvoronov1988geometry','beilinsonmanin1986mumford'),cls='A',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, the determinant-line and supergeometric constructions are established. The notebook formulas remain schematic and do not constitute a complete measure or a finiteness theorem.')
item(707,'P001371','remark','A proposed noncommutative Ray--Singer torsion',r'The final proposal is a noncommutative analogue of Ray--Singer torsion, explicitly accompanied by ``no promise yet''.',r'No coefficient algebra, trace, elliptic complex, determinant convention or invariance statement is supplied. Several inequivalent noncommutative torsion theories could fit the phrase; none is singled out by the record.',r'The sentence is preserved as a proposal without assigning it to an arbitrary later torsion construction. The nearby string determinant theory is thematic context, not an exact solution.',(),cls='F',correctness='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',development='none',confidence='low',status=r'As of 12 November 2026, this particular proposal is not precisely identifiable from the notebook. It is not classified D merely because the exact referent was not found.')
correction('P001369','ordinary conformal derivative','partial_tilde=F partial with F=d z_tilde/dz','Use partial_tilde=F^(-1) partial, or reverse the definition of F.','The evidence block and manuscript preserve both formulas; the ordinary chain rule supplies the reciprocal.')
correction('P001369','supermoduli dimension','(3g-3|2g-2) without genus qualification','State unpunctured genus g>=2; include automorphism and puncture corrections in other cases.','Witten equations (2.26)-(2.27) distinguish the dimension from the automorphism-corrected formula.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P001371','Schwarz versus Sinai catalogue sequence','record 707 before record 708','Retain catalogue record numbers and dates, while placing the Sinai material on P001368-P001369 before the explicit Schwarz heading on P001369.','Manuscript speaker headings determine the actual order.','both','provenance')
